The `!excluded.contains(dept)` predicate is true when the department is NOT in the exclusion set.

### Why use Set instead of List for the exclusion check?

| Operation | HashSet | ArrayList |
|---|---|---|
| contains() time | O(1) average | O(n) linear scan |
| 10 excluded depts, 10000 employees | 10000 hash lookups | 10000 x 10 comparisons |
| Memory | Slightly more (hash table overhead) | Slightly less (array) |
| Best for | Membership checks | Indexed access |

```java
// WRONG: List.contains scans linearly
List<String> excludedList = List.of("IT", "HR", "ADMIN", "FACILITIES");
List<Employee> slow = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> !excludedList.contains(e.getDepartment()))  // O(n) per check
    .toList();

// CORRECT: Set.contains is O(1) average
Set<String> excludedSet = Set.of("IT", "HR", "ADMIN", "FACILITIES");
List<Employee> fast = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> !excludedSet.contains(e.getDepartment()))  // O(1) per check
    .toList();
```

### How do you use Predicate.not() for cleaner negation?

Java 11 added `Predicate.not()` which can make negations more readable, though for this pattern the lambda is usually clearer.

```java
import java.util.function.Predicate;

// Lambda negation (most common)
List<Employee> lambda = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> !excluded.contains(e.getDepartment()))
    .toList();

// Predicate.not with a named predicate
Predicate<Employee> inExcluded = e -> excluded.contains(e.getDepartment());
List<Employee> negated = employees.stream()
    .filter(Predicate.not(inExcluded))
    .toList();

// negate() instance method
List<Employee> negate = employees.stream()
    .filter(inExcluded.negate())
    .toList();
```

### How do you handle case-insensitive department matching?

```java
// Build the exclusion set with normalized case
Set<String> excludedUpper = Set.of("IT", "HR");

// Normalize the compared value to match
List<Employee> caseInsensitive = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> !excludedUpper.contains(
        e.getDepartment().toUpperCase()))
    .toList();
```

### What are common mistakes?

Mistake 1: building the Set inside the predicate.

```java
// WRONG: creates a new Set for every employee!
List<Employee> wrong = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> !Set.of("IT", "HR").contains(e.getDepartment()))
    .toList();

// CORRECT: build once outside
Set<String> excluded = Set.of("IT", "HR");
List<Employee> correct = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> !excluded.contains(e.getDepartment()))
    .toList();
```

Mistake 2: using equals instead of contains for multiple values.

```java
// WRONG: only checks one department, misses the other
List<Employee> wrong = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> !e.getDepartment().equals("IT")
              && !e.getDepartment().equals("HR"))
    .toList();
// Becomes unreadable with 5+ departments

// CORRECT: Set.contains handles any number of exclusions
Set<String> excluded = Set.of("IT", "HR", "ADMIN", "FACILITIES", "LEGAL");
List<Employee> correct = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> !excluded.contains(e.getDepartment()))
    .toList();
```

### What about including instead of excluding?

Flip the negation to filter for departments that ARE in the set.

```java
Set<String> included = Set.of("TRADING", "RISK");

// Include: keep only TRADING and RISK
List<Employee> included = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> included.contains(e.getDepartment()))
    .toList();

// Exclude: keep everything EXCEPT TRADING and RISK
List<Employee> excluded = employees.stream()
    .filter(e -> !included.contains(e.getDepartment()))
    .toList();
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "What if departments is an enum?" Use `EnumSet` for faster membership checks.
- "How do you exclude by multiple fields?" Create a composite key: `excluded.contains(e.getDepartment() + "|" + e.getCity())`.
- "What if the excluded set changes at runtime?" Use a `ConcurrentHashMap.newKeySet()` for thread-safe mutation.
- "How do you log which employees were excluded?" Use `peek(e -> log(e))` before the filter, or partition with `partitioningBy` to get both included and excluded lists.
